WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:01.322
Watch closely.

2
00:00:01.711 --> 00:00:05.422
Every time this single-photon detector
fires, it goes blind.

3
00:00:05.811 --> 00:00:09.656
And every photon that arrives during that
blind window is gone forever.

4
00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:12.460
This is MaRS, the Markov-Renewal

5
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.256
Single-Photon LiDAR Simulator, presented
at ECCV 2026.

6
00:00:16.811 --> 00:00:21.011
A single-photon LiDAR fires a pulse and
times individual returning photons.

7
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.733
Over many cycles each pixel builds a
timing histogram, and the array builds a
cube.

8
00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:28.289
Here is what that blindness does to the
data.

9
00:00:28.678 --> 00:00:31.248
The recorded histogram is not a scaled
copy of the

10
00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:33.767
true one: the peak is clipped and pulled
earlier.

11
00:00:34.156 --> 00:00:37.733
And the recorded count is far smaller,
with a variance nothing like Poisson.

12
00:00:38.122 --> 00:00:40.567
Any network trained on this learns the
wrong physics.

13
00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:42.700
So how do you simulate that cube?

14
00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:45.642
A Poisson model is fast, but it ignores
dead

15
00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:48.311
time, so under high flux the physics is
wrong.

16
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.123
The sequential gold standard is correct,
but

17
00:00:51.123 --> 00:00:53.767
it examines every photon, and it does not
scale.

18
00:00:54.322 --> 00:00:57.567
Our key idea is to factor the histogram
into two parts.

19
00:00:57.956 --> 00:01:00.489
A count: how many photons actually
register.

20
00:01:00.878 --> 00:01:02.944
And a shape: where in time they land.

21
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.733
Earlier work solved the shape.

22
00:01:05.122 --> 00:01:06.944
The count under dead time was open.

23
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.644
MaRS solves the count.

24
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.822
Here is why the count is hard.

25
00:01:11.211 --> 00:01:13.442
Dead time couples each detection to the
next:

26
00:01:13.442 --> 00:01:16.567
detections are neither independent nor
identically distributed.

27
00:01:16.956 --> 00:01:20.133
So we propose a formulation built for
exactly that coupling.

28
00:01:20.522 --> 00:01:23.300
An embedded chain says where the next
photon lands.

29
00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:25.178
A holding time says when.

30
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.767
Together: a Markov renewal process.

31
00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:29.767
And here is the key insight.

32
00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.711
Counting is the inverse of waiting.

33
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.266
The embedded chain mixes quickly, so the

34
00:01:34.266 --> 00:01:37.189
cumulative waiting time obeys a central
limit theorem.

35
00:01:37.578 --> 00:01:40.067
Inverting that relationship gives the
photon count.

36
00:01:40.456 --> 00:01:44.389
The first closed form count distribution
under dead time, with no per photon loop.

37
00:01:44.944 --> 00:01:48.300
The variance is an infinite series of
covariance terms.

38
00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:53.422
They decay at wildly different rates, so
any fixed cutoff is biased or expensive.

39
00:01:53.811 --> 00:01:57.767
Instead we sum the tail in closed form,
from five dominant eigenmodes.

40
00:01:58.156 --> 00:01:59.578
Twelve times lower error.

41
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:00.844
Does it work?

42
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:04.336
Against the gold standard, our count
distribution matches

43
00:02:04.336 --> 00:02:07.656
in centre and spread, where earlier models
miss the variance.

44
00:02:08.044 --> 00:02:12.578
KL divergence 0.013 — thirty times better
than the best prior model.

45
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:15.526
And on a real SPAD with an 85 ns dead
time,

46
00:02:15.526 --> 00:02:18.144
our prediction lands on the measured
counts.

47
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.300
So the statistics are right.

48
00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:23.222
Now they have to be fast enough to make
training data.

49
00:02:23.778 --> 00:02:26.378
Because everything is closed form, we can
precompute it.

50
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:30.567
Each entry stores the count's mean and
variance, and the whole temporal PDF.

51
00:02:30.956 --> 00:02:33.756
Those depend only on signal, background
and delay.

52
00:02:34.144 --> 00:02:37.063
But delay has to be sampled at full timing

53
00:02:37.063 --> 00:02:40.122
resolution, so even a modest grid is 108
GB.

54
00:02:40.678 --> 00:02:41.833
Then we noticed something.

55
00:02:42.222 --> 00:02:45.444
Changing the delay only shifts the
registered PDF — circularly.

56
00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:47.544
The mean and the variance do not move.

57
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:53.178
So delay drops out of the table: 108 GB
becomes 105 MB.

58
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:55.067
Here is the whole simulator.

59
00:02:55.456 --> 00:02:58.522
Reflectivity sets the signal flux; depth
sets the delay.

60
00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:02.889
One batched lookup gives every pixel its
mean, variance and temporal shape.

61
00:03:03.278 --> 00:03:05.678
A circular shift moves each peak to its
own depth.

62
00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:10.156
One Gaussian draw gives the count, one
multinomial spreads it over the bins.

63
00:03:10.544 --> 00:03:12.256
No per photon loop anywhere.

64
00:03:12.811 --> 00:03:13.744
Now the payoff.

65
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:20.578
One histogram cube, 128×128×1024: 40.8 s
for the gold standard.

66
00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.256
MaRS, 1.01 s.

67
00:03:23.644 --> 00:03:27.644
40× faster — that is what makes training
data at scale possible.

68
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.267
So we can ask what an accurate count model
is actually worth.

69
00:03:31.656 --> 00:03:34.656
The Poisson simulator gets both the count
and the shape wrong.

70
00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:38.711
Renewal pairs the right shape with a count
whose mean and variance are both off.

71
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.900
Zhang et al. gets the mean right, but not
the variance.

72
00:03:42.456 --> 00:03:43.811
And it matters downstream.

73
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.333
We train the same network on each
simulator,

74
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:48.467
then test them all on gold standard
physics.

75
00:03:48.856 --> 00:03:54.411
The Poisson-trained network collapses — 7
dB, and a depth error of 1.65 m.

76
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.689
MaRS transfers almost perfectly.

77
00:03:57.078 --> 00:03:59.989
Smallest gap, and the best absolute result
on both.

78
00:04:00.544 --> 00:04:02.944
30× closer to the true photon-count law.

79
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:05.333
1000× smaller lookup table.

80
00:04:05.722 --> 00:04:07.678
40× faster than the gold standard.

81
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.800
And 17 dB better downstream
reconstruction.

82
00:04:11.356 --> 00:04:12.644
A few honest limits.

83
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:15.029
We assume a nonparalyzable detector,

84
00:04:15.029 --> 00:04:18.078
negligible afterpulsing, and a single
return per pixel.

85
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:22.600
A GPU version, a compact table, and
paralyzable detectors are next.

86
00:04:23.156 --> 00:04:26.756
The paper, the code, and the project page
are all linked below.

87
00:04:27.144 --> 00:04:28.144
Thank you for watching.
